import Sql from '../../components/explainers/querying/Sql.astro';
import AcceptanceFigure from '../../components/relational-next/a32/AcceptanceFigure.astro';
import worksheetUrl from '../../components/relational-next/a32/acceptance.py?url&no-inline';
const sections = [
 ['put-the-database-file-in-the-deployment-diagram','Put the database file in the deployment diagram'],
 ['observe-two-connections-competing-for-a-write','Observe two connections competing for a write'],
 ['choose-a-journal-and-durability-contract','Choose a journal and durability contract'],
 ['prove-the-application-s-data-contract-on-sqlite','Prove the application’s data contract on SQLite'],
 ['rehearse-a-recoverable-deployment','Rehearse a recoverable deployment'],
 ['make-the-adoption-decision-from-acceptance-evidence','Make the adoption decision from acceptance evidence'],
];
const setup = `import sqlite3
print(sqlite3.sqlite_version)        # Library loaded into this application
print(sqlite3.threadsafety)
c = sqlite3.connect("shop.db", autocommit=True, timeout=0.08)
# Bootstrap WAL once, without other active transactions; verify its result.
assert c.execute("PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL").fetchone()[0] == "wal"
# Initialise and read back on EVERY new connection, before BEGIN.
c.execute("PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON")
c.execute("PRAGMA synchronous=FULL")
c.execute("PRAGMA busy_timeout=80")
for name in ("journal_mode", "foreign_keys", "synchronous",
             "busy_timeout", "wal_autocheckpoint"):
    print(name, c.execute("PRAGMA " + name).fetchone()[0])`;
const schema = `CREATE TABLE inventory (
  sku TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  available INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (available >= 0)
) STRICT;
CREATE TABLE reservations (
  request_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  sku TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES inventory(sku),
  cents INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (cents > 0),
  placed TEXT NOT NULL,
  note TEXT
) STRICT;`;
const transaction = `# Boundary illustration: new request only; replay resolution is separate.
c.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
try:
    changed = c.execute("""UPDATE inventory
      SET available=available-1 WHERE sku=? AND available>0""", ("P7",))
    if changed.rowcount != 1:
        raise ValueError("No stock claimed")
    c.execute("INSERT INTO reservations VALUES(?,?,?,?,?)",
              ("R1", "P7", 2000, "2026-10-20T12:00:00Z", None))
    c.execute("COMMIT")
except BaseException:
    if c.in_transaction:
        c.execute("ROLLBACK")
    raise`;
---

<p>SQLite runs inside the application and stores a relational database in local files. That removes a database server to provision and a network round trip from each query. It also puts file ownership, writer coordination and recovery in the application’s deployment contract.</p>
<p>We will check a single-host version of the shop’s reservation service: two web-worker processes and one background job open the same local database. One reservation reduces stock and records a request identity together. The <a href="/review/relational-next/choosing/">parent’s three-host shop</a> cannot directly share a SQLite WAL file; this is an explicitly changed deployment for a disposable prototype. Its production promises have not disappeared.</p>
<p>The small checks below ran on 20 October 2026 through Python 3.14.5’s standard <code>sqlite3</code> driver, loading SQLite 3.54.0 on macOS 26.6.2 arm64. Figures explain causes; labelled observations come from that run. No production workload, power failure or host-loss recovery was tested.</p>

