<h2 id="put-the-database-file-in-the-deployment-diagram">Put the database file in the deployment diagram</h2>
<p>Draw every process that opens the file, including maintenance and backup tools. Our workers have separate connections but one stock authority. The service owner supplies a persistent local volume, protects its directory and owns replacement after failure. An off-host recovery copy needs its own owner and access path.</p>
<p>Advance Figure 1 to opening the files, then change the job’s host. At the last step, ask which copy survives losing the host.</p>
<AcceptanceFigure scene="ownership" number={1} />
<p><a href="https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html">Standard SQLite WAL requires processes on the same host</a>. A network-mounted directory is not a way to give several application hosts a database service. Placing a single writer behind an API changes the architecture and adds service recovery duties. If independent hosts must directly access one shared database, reject this arrangement at the deployment screen.</p>

<h3 id="verify-the-library-the-application-actually-loads">Verify the library the application actually loads</h3>
<p>The installed command-line tool reported SQLite 3.51.0; Python loaded 3.54.0, with source ID dated 9 October 2026. Running SQL in the former would not test the latter. Record the application image, Python and SQLite versions, <code>SELECT sqlite_source_id()</code>, <code>PRAGMA compile_options</code>, required modules and supported platforms. Our fixture needs STRICT tables and exercises JSON extraction; neither an FTS build flag nor this JSON result proves an untested extension works.</p>
<p>The driver reported <code>threadsafety=3</code>, but its default <code>check_same_thread</code> still restricts a connection to its creating thread. This run uses separate connections in each process, never an inherited live connection. Verify your framework’s ownership and pooling rules against <a href="https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/sqlite3.html#sqlite3.connect">the driver documentation</a>. Changing a thread check does not define who owns a transaction.</p>
<p>Read release qualifications too. SQLite documents a <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html#walresetbug">WAL-reset race fixed in 3.51.3 and later</a>, with specified backports. The loaded 3.54.0 passes that documented version screen; the installed 3.51.0 CLI is not a substitute for our tested runtime.</p>

<h3 id="check-the-storage-and-access-boundary">Check the storage and access boundary</h3>
<p>The running file set includes the database, a <code>-wal</code> file holding committed changes and a <code>-shm</code> coordination file. Workers need appropriate file and directory access to create and maintain these sidecars. Keep them together under SQLite’s control. Manually deleting WAL to reclaim space can discard committed data.</p>
<p>The disposable test used a private temporary directory with mode 0700 on local storage. That proves neither container restart persistence nor the production volume’s locking and sync behaviour. Mount the intended persistent volume, run as the intended service identity and rehearse container replacement with existing data. Check directory permissions as well as file permissions. Access to the process and files is the protection boundary; use <a href="/review/relational-next/security/">Protecting relational data</a> for credentials, encryption, recovery keys and the wider threat model.</p>

<h2 id="observe-two-connections-competing-for-a-write">Observe two connections competing for a write</h2>
<p>SQLite permits one write transaction at a time for a database. Twenty quick independent changes may fit comfortably; one change held open during a slow remote call can block every other writer. Database size and HTTP request count alone cannot settle that question.</p>
<p>In the observed WAL schedule, connection A held <code>BEGIN IMMEDIATE</code>. Connection B’s same command returned <code>SQLITE_BUSY</code>. A separate Python process opening the same file met the same error. Both had an 80 ms busy setting; elapsed wall time exceeded 80 ms in this run. After A rolled back, a fresh attempt succeeded. In a separate schedule the holder released while another connection waited, and that attempt succeeded.</p>

<h3 id="choose-when-a-transaction-asks-to-write">Choose when a transaction asks to write</h3>
<p><a href="https://www.sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html">BEGIN IMMEDIATE</a> asks for the writer at the transaction’s start. A deferred transaction can first establish a read snapshot and ask to write later. In WAL, if another writer commits in between, promoting the old snapshot would fork database history. SQLite rejects it with <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/isolation.html">SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT</a>.</p>
<p>Step through the immediate case, then switch to the deferred case at the second step. Watch who owns writer access and which stock value A can still see.</p>
<AcceptanceFigure scene="writer" number={2} />
<p>Our deferred reader saw stock 6 before and after another connection committed 5. Its write then failed with extended code 517, <code>SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT</code>, immediately rather than consuming the busy wait. End that transaction and repeat its reads and decisions under the application’s retry policy. A larger timeout cannot refresh an existing snapshot. <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a> owns the invariant argument; <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">Recovering requests and uncertain outcomes</a> owns repeatable request identities and outcomes.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.sqlite.org/c3ref/busy_timeout.html">Busy handling</a> is a bounded opportunity to wait, not a guarantee that every write will run or a hard end-to-end deadline. Set one policy per connection; account for queueing and application retries inside the original request budget. Record errors and refused attempts as well as successful latency.</p>
<p>Make driver transaction control explicit. The worksheet selects Python <code>autocommit=True</code> and sends SQL <code>BEGIN</code>, <code>COMMIT</code> and <code>ROLLBACK</code>; Python’s <code>commit()</code> and <code>rollback()</code> do nothing in that mode. Its separately tested legacy default left a transaction closed after SELECT but open after INSERT. Python’s <code>autocommit=False</code> instead maintains a transaction. Those choices change when the application holds resources.</p>

<h3 id="keep-reader-and-writer-lifetimes-visible">Keep reader and writer lifetimes visible</h3>
<p>Finish or close cursors and end transactions deliberately. A response streamed slowly from an open cursor can extend a reader beyond the time spent executing its query. Fetch a bounded page and release the database before waiting on the client where the application contract permits. Keep email delivery and other remote work outside the stock transaction; <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a> owns that boundary.</p>
<p>A long WAL reader need not block a writer, but can prevent checkpoint progress and log reuse. Figure 3 starts a reader, appends eight commits, then attempts a checkpoint. At the last step, end the reader and compare.</p>
<AcceptanceFigure scene="checkpoint" number={3} />
<p>With automatic checkpointing temporarily disabled, our pinned reader left eight WAL frames, 32,992 bytes, and PASSIVE returned <code>(0, 8, 0)</code>: no busy flag, eight log frames, none checkpointed. After the reader ended, TRUNCATE returned <code>(0, 0, 0)</code> and the WAL was zero bytes. <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/c3ref/wal_checkpoint_v2.html">PASSIVE does not wait</a> for readers; inspect progress rather than interpreting its zero busy flag as completion. Restore the checkpoint policy after this experiment.</p>
<p>Measure these lifetimes with the actual number of worker processes, background jobs, slow readers and overlapping writes. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">the common workload method</a> for representative volume, skew, response percentiles and errors. This scheduled demonstration establishes behaviour, not the shop’s production capacity.</p>

<h2 id="choose-a-journal-and-durability-contract">Choose a journal and durability contract</h2>
<p>A rollback journal preserves old pages before the database changes them. Readers can delay the writer’s commit. In our rollback-mode comparison, a reader stayed open while another connection updated a row; COMMIT returned busy and left the write transaction active. Ending the reader let that same COMMIT finish. WAL instead appends new pages and lets an existing reader keep its snapshot, with checkpoint work to account for.</p>
<p>WAL therefore fits our prototype’s overlapping reads and short writes, but still has one writer. Read-mostly applications and deployment constraints may favour rollback journalling. Compare the actual paths rather than treating WAL as an automatic upgrade.</p>

<h3 id="state-what-a-successful-commit-must-survive">State what a successful commit must survive</h3>
<p>Separate an application process being killed, the operating system losing its memory and the whole volume disappearing. The first leaves the operating system alive; a successful process-kill rehearsal cannot establish power-loss durability. A lost volume needs a copy elsewhere regardless of local journal settings.</p>
<p>In Figure 4, advance to the successful reply and switch from FULL to NORMAL. The application has heard the same answer; what has reached durable storage differs.</p>
<AcceptanceFigure scene="durability" number={4} />
<p><a href="https://www.sqlite.org/pragma.html#pragma_synchronous">SQLite’s documented durability matrix</a> makes WAL with FULL the starting contract for acknowledged reservations: it syncs WAL at commit. WAL with NORMAL can lose recent acknowledged transactions after OS or power failure while remaining consistent. In rollback DELETE mode, EXTRA adds directory synchronisation and is the documented choice when power-loss durability is required. OFF removes safeguards needed for this application.</p>
<p>These promises depend on the filesystem, operating system and device honouring sync. Our macOS run read <code>fullfsync=0</code> and <code>checkpoint_fullfsync=0</code>; it did not verify the storage flush contract or test macOS’s stronger full-sync option. Production acceptance must resolve that platform-specific contract. Our small successful commits establish no power-loss result.</p>
<Sql code={setup} label="Prototype starting connection policy; use a fresh disposable file when practising" />
<p>WAL mode persists in the database. Initialise foreign keys, synchronous policy and busy handling on every new connection, outside a transaction, and read them back. Our two connections both reported WAL, synchronous 2, foreign keys 1, busy timeout 80, 4,096-byte pages and automatic checkpoint threshold 1,000 pages. Treat that threshold as checkpoint policy, not a guaranteed maximum log size. Service recovery remains with <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a>.</p>

<h2 id="prove-the-application-s-data-contract-on-sqlite">Prove the application’s data contract on SQLite</h2>
<p>Use the schema and critical statements through the intended driver. Here money is integer minor units, the request identity is supplied before the attempt, and stock cannot be negative. The wider design methods remain in <a href="/review/relational-next/data-design/">Designing a relational schema</a>.</p>
<Sql code={schema} label="The small acceptance schema; time formatting is an application contract" />
<p><a href="https://www.sqlite.org/foreignkeys.html">Foreign-key enforcement</a> must be enabled on each connection, before BEGIN. Verify an unknown SKU is rejected on newly opened connections. <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/stricttables.html">STRICT tables</a> constrain stored types but allow lossless conversions: our numeric text <code>"2000"</code> became integer 2000, whereas <code>"twenty"</code> was rejected with code 3091. STRICT does not validate a timestamp string’s business meaning.</p>
<p>The observed fixture accepted R1 with 2,000 cents, UTC text and NULL note. It rejected negative stock, an unknown SKU, NULL money and a repeated request key. The duplicate error exposed a more important boundary: a prior stock decrement remained active until the application rolled back.</p>
<p>Advance Figure 5 to the duplicate, then compare the handler’s final action. The wrong branch leaves the constraint working and the application wrong.</p>
<AcceptanceFigure scene="contract" number={5} />
<Sql code={transaction} label="Rollback the whole new-request attempt after failure; not a complete replay handler" />
<p>In the real duplicate test, stock was 5 inside the still-open failed attempt and returned to 6 after rollback; R1 remained the sole reservation. This proves the tested boundary, not the complete request-recovery protocol. Resolve an existing request and compare its original arguments before deciding what a repeat means.</p>

<h3 id="check-representations-at-the-driver-boundary">Check representations at the driver boundary</h3>
<p>Python round-tripped <code>9223372036854775807</code> as an integer; binding the next integer raised <code>OverflowError</code> before SQL execution. Integer cents keep this fixture exact; establish currency, scale, range and arithmetic overflow rules for the real application. Do not assume declaring DECIMAL supplies an exact fixed-scale decimal type in SQLite.</p>
<p>The UTC timestamp text and NULL note returned unchanged. This schema does not reject malformed dates, so validate or constrain the representation your application requires. Test offsets, ordering and boundary instants through its real conversion path. Default adapters are no substitute for an explicit time contract.</p>
<p>Our comparison returned true for <code>A=a COLLATE NOCASE</code> and false for <code>Æ=æ COLLATE NOCASE</code>. SQLite’s <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/datatype3.html#collation">built-in NOCASE folds ASCII letters</a>; it is not a Unicode case-folding rule. Required Unicode uniqueness or sorting needs a deliberately supported and tested policy. Check actual SQL, generated values, JSON and extension calls, then rehearse required schema changes with <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a>.</p>

<h2 id="rehearse-a-recoverable-deployment">Rehearse a recoverable deployment</h2>
<p>Use a supported consistent copy mechanism, such as <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/backup.html">SQLite’s online backup API</a>. Python exposes it as <code>source.backup(destination)</code>. Check completion and independently reopen the destination. A file copied from a changing main database does not establish a consistent recovery point, especially when acknowledged changes remain in WAL.</p>
<p>At Figure 6’s copy step, compare the API with copying just the main file. Then follow the destination through validation.</p>
<AcceptanceFigure scene="restore" number={6} />
<p>Our API copy and independent reopen preserved the ordered reservation rows, stock 6, one reservation worth 2,000 cents and nine event rows. <code>integrity_check</code> returned <code>ok</code> and <code>foreign_key_check</code> returned no rows. Both checks matter: structural integrity alone does not establish application invariants.</p>
<p>The harness timed the tiny local backup, reopen and validation in its evidence output. That clock excluded provisioning, remote transfer, credentials, production volume and returning the service to users. The destination was on the same host. It proves no off-host survival or production recovery time. Apply <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">the shared restoration method</a> to a protected copy outside the named failure boundary; reconcile every independently recorded acknowledged identity and measure the complete recovery clock.</p>

<h3 id="verify-restarts-upgrades-and-interrupted-work">Verify restarts, upgrades and interrupted work</h3>
<p>The worksheet killed only its own child after that child inserted an uncommitted event. Reopening retained all nine committed events, omitted the unfinished event and passed integrity checking. It also committed an added status column with a default, reopened the data and read the existing reservation. That checks one interrupted transaction and one additive change; rolling upgrades with old and new application versions remain untested.</p>
<p>A read-only URI refused a write with <code>SQLITE_READONLY</code>. A separate database limited with <code>max_page_count</code> refused a large insert with <code>SQLITE_FULL</code>, left no payload rows and passed integrity checking. The latter is a page-budget failure, not physical disk exhaustion. No I/O-failure injection, power cut, lost volume or OS permission denial was performed.</p>
<p>Retain extended error codes and transaction state. Errors can abort a statement or the whole transaction; clean up the owning connection explicitly and verify state before reuse. Rehearse the actual service’s error response, restart, persistent mount, schema-version check and connection initialisation. Use the operations and live-change articles for the full rollout and recovery procedures.</p>

<h2 id="make-the-adoption-decision-from-acceptance-evidence">Make the adoption decision from acceptance evidence</h2>
<p><strong>Accept SQLite 3.54.0 through the recorded Python driver for this disposable, single-host prototype.</strong> Use WAL, FULL synchronous policy, verified foreign keys on every connection, explicit short write transactions and bounded busy handling as the starting configuration. The accepted boundary is the small exercised fixture and local storage arrangement.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="SQLite adoption evidence and remaining gates"><table><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Evidence status</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Several hosts directly share WAL</td><td>Documented same-host requirement.</td><td>Reject the parent’s original arrangement.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Driver, settings and critical data</td><td>Observed loaded version, two initialised connections, accepted/rejected values and rollback invariants.</td><td>Pass this compact prototype check.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Overlapping connections and processes</td><td>Observed busy outcomes, wait then success, stale snapshot and pinned-reader checkpoint.</td><td>Behaviour established; production latency and error-rate gates untested.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Restart and local recovery</td><td>Observed child termination, additive change, backup/reopen and supported error cases.</td><td>Pass those limited cases. I/O failure and deployment-image coexistence remain gaps.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Power, host loss and production recovery</td><td>Durability documentation; sync contract, protected remote copies and full recovery clock untested.</td><td>Defer production adoption. One local volume cannot establish the parent’s zero acknowledged host-loss gate.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Reopen the choice when another host must own writes, writer overlap exceeds the measured deadline/error budget, readers prevent acceptable WAL reuse, recovery requirements change or the deployed runtime changes. Include volume, backups and operator effort in cost. If those gates cannot pass economically, return to <a href="/review/relational-next/choosing/">the selection decision</a>; a future engine move follows <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">the common cutover method</a>. The <a href="/review/relational-next/cockroachdb/">CockroachDB check</a> is an alternative route only when a distributed requirement justifies it.</p>
<p>To repeat the bounded cases, <a href={worksheetUrl} download="sqlite-acceptance.py">save the SQLite acceptance worksheet</a>. Use Python 3.12 or later with its standard sqlite3 module, a loaded SQLite supporting STRICT tables, WAL and JSON, and a local writable temporary directory, then run <code>python3 sqlite-acceptance.py &gt; sqlite-evidence.json</code>. Our observed command used <code>/opt/homebrew/bin/python3</code>. The script creates and removes private databases, spawns and reaps only copies of itself, and prints versions, settings, raw outcomes and timings. It never opens an existing database. Run the equivalent acceptance cases in the intended application image and storage environment before transferring any conclusion.</p>

